  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1246 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nGlobal models and ensemble guidance suggest that the mid level ridge \ncontinues to strengthen during the long term thus reducing our \nchances for rain and storms. It eventually pushes off to the east \nacross the TX/LA border by the end of the week before meandering \nback west southwestward over our area over the weekend. The result \nis most areas should remain dry with just very spotty isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms across the Coastal Plains and again across \nthe Rio Grande as they remain on the periphery of the ridge. The \ngood news is that with the ridge being close by it should drop our \nhumidity values beginning Thursday as onshore flow from the Gulf \nwill be reduced. While it will remain unseasonably hot across the \narea, heat indices will only likely be 1 to 3 degrees above the \nactual air temperatures giving us some reprieve from the oppressive \nheat and humidity we have experienced for most of this month. \nUnfortunately rain chances continue to look abysmal for most of the \narea as long as the ridge remains parked nearby. There is some hope \nin the long term as long range models are starting to indicate a \npattern shift thus favoring our area for a more active weather \npattern as we end September and head into October.",
